# The chrome raster they replace. The app ICONS are a different set of files
# and a different session's work — untouched here.
rm -f together-city-react/public/assets/img/tc-lockup.webp
echo "== artwork written, raster removed"

cd together-city-react
npx tsc --noEmit
npx vitest run
node scripts/a11y-audit.mjs
node scripts/motion-ceiling.mjs
node scripts/lint-ceiling.mjs
npx vite build
cd ..

git add together-city-react/src/styles/relief.css \
        together-city-react/src/layouts/Header.tsx \
        together-city-react/public/assets/img/tc-mark.svg \
        together-city-react/public/assets/img/tc-word.svg \
        together-city-react/public/assets/img/tc-lockup.webp
git commit -F - <<'MSG'
The mark takes the corner and the name takes the middle

The owner supplied a hand-lettered TC monogram and a hand-lettered "Together
City" in the same hand, and asked for the monogram in the top left corner with
the name in the middle. They replace the chrome raster lockup in the header,
and the reasoning that came with that lockup goes with it: there is no shading
to keep continuous now, no white-on-white reading by its shadow, and no
argument to have about whether type could imitate it. A signature cannot be set
in a typeface, so this one is not.

This is the HEADER. The app icons that landed earlier today are a different set
of files and a different piece of work; nothing here touches them.

TWO FILES, NOT ONE, WHICH REVERSES AN ARGUMENT MADE AN HOUR EARLIER. The
composed single-SVG lockup was right on the reasoning that two tags kept in
step by a CSS gap drift apart. That argument holds only while the two are
touching. Here the relationship between them IS the layout — corner and centre
at full width, side by side below 1100px — and a composed image cannot come
apart.

THE MARK IS OUT OF FLOW, WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON THE NAME IS ACTUALLY CENTRED.
`.tc-header-top` centres its contents. A monogram sitting in flow beside the
word would centre the PAIR, leaving the name right of centre by half a
monogram — the kind of wrong that looks fine in a diff and wrong on a screen.
Absolute, pinned to the row's left edge — the same trick `.tc-burger` uses two
rules above — takes it out of the centring calculation. `left: 0` is the row's
content edge, which is also where the first hub tab begins: the mark and
ASTROLOGY share a left margin rather than being two near-misses.

AND IT COMES BACK INTO FLOW AT 1100px, BECAUSE THAT IS WHERE THE BURGER
APPEARS. Below that width the burger owns the left corner and a mark pinned
there is drawn on top of it. That was not reasoned about — it was rendered,
seen, and fixed: the first cut used 1023px, the breakpoint that hides the nav,
and put the monogram straight through the burger at 1100 and 1050. Checked at
1440, 1200, 1100, 1000, 880 and 390.

BOTH ARE SVG BECAUSE BOTH ARE PEN STROKES. Traced, they are 2.9 KB and 10.7 KB
of path data — together smaller than one 1x raster of the same drawing, and
correct at every size from 26px in a phone header to whatever a 3x display asks
for, with no 2x, no 3x and nothing to re-export when a clamp moves. They also
land inside an exemption that already existed: relief.css excuses
`img[src$=".svg"]` from the global outline-and-shadow every other image wears,
so neither needed a rule of its own to stop being framed like a photograph.

THIRTY-FOUR PIXELS IS THE CEILING BECAUSE IT IS THE ROW. Row 1 of the header is
34px inside a header fixed at 78px; a taller mark does not make the header
taller, it overhangs Row 2 and crowds the tabs. Measured in the real cascade at
1440: mark 42x34 at the corner, word 77x30 on the centre line, tabs starting
three pixels below.

ONE ALT BETWEEN THEM. The mark carries `alt=""` and the word carries the name,
because this is the same name said twice and a screen reader should hear the
city once, not stutter it.

The invert for astrology and entertainment stays, on a different argument than
the chrome lockup used. A modelled object earned it by staying modelled under a
flip; a flat line drawing earns it because the stroke is uniform, so inverting
produces exactly the drawing the same pen would have made on black paper.
Rendered against the astrology ground before this landed, not assumed.

AND A NOTE ON THE TITLE. The first three cuts of this script announced
themselves as "The city signs its own name" and would have no-opped forever:
another session committed the app-icon work under that exact subject while this
was being built. Two pieces of work, one title, and the one that lands second
becomes invisible. Land scripts key on the subject line, so a subject is an
identifier, not a headline.

tsc clean, vitest all green, a11y 0, motion at ceiling, lint at ceiling, vite
build clean.

MSG
echo "== committed"

echo
echo "==============================================================="
echo " Landed: $NEEDS — push and Vercel ships it."
echo " Monogram in the top left corner, the name on the centre line,"
echo " and the two ride together below 1100px where the burger is."
echo " The app icons and the APK are still uncommitted; they belong"
echo " to another session's work and were deliberately left alone."
echo "==============================================================="
